still a relatively new concept at the time."},{"id":"design_deficiency","role":"contributing","evidence":"These systems were designed to be redundant, such that if two hydraulic systems were inoperable, the one remaining hydraulic system would still permit the full operation and control of the airplane."},{"id":"engine_instrument_error","role":"contributing","evidence":"A majority of those aboard survived; experienced test pilots in simulators were unable to reproduce a survivable landing."},{"id":"fatigue","role":"contributing","evidence":"The DC-10 is designed so that if hydraulic pressure to the landing gear is lost, the gear will fall down slightly and rest on the landing gear doors."},{"id":"flight_control_failure","role":"contributing","evidence":"This lever has the added benefit of unlocking the outboard ailerons, which are not used in high-speed flight and are locked in a neutral position."},{"id":"hydraulic_failure","role":"contributing","evidence":"Like other widebody transport aircraft of the time, the DC-10 was not designed to revert to unassisted manual control in the event of total hydraulic failure."},{"id":"inadequate_training","role":"contributing","evidence":"Denny Fitch, 46, was a training check pilot who was on Flight 232 as a passenger."},{"id":"inflight_fire","role":"contributing","evidence":"Fire trucks had been placed on Runway 22, anticipating a landing on nearby Runway 31, so all the vehicles were quickly moved out of the way before the airplane touched down."},{"id":"known_defect_unresolved","role":"contributing","evidence":"It has been termed \"The Impossible Landing\" as it is considered one of the most impressive landings performed in the history of aviation."}],"chain":[["emergency_landing","known_defect_unresolved"],["crm_breakdown","known_defect_unresolved"]],"events":[{"t":0,"clock":null,"phase":"takeoff","actor":"SYS","kind":"system","text":"Aircraft departed runway","factors":[]},{"t":300,"clock":null,"phase":"initial_climb","acto